Q: Why does this return statement seem to require a const_cast? A: <blockquote>
<p>I was under the impression that the implementer of the class could do whatever they wanted and that it was merely the case that const objects could only call const functions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A const object of any class type can only be safely modified inside the constructor of that respective class. In all other circumstances, it is treated as a const(read only) object. So, even though casting away constness in itself not undefined behavior but if one attempt to modify the const object via casting away its constness that will constitute UB.</p>
<p>Here is a more simplified version to clarify this, also note the use of <code>mutable</code>:</p>
<pre><code>struct C
{
   int x;
   mutable int y;
   C()
   {
    x= 4; //this works even for const objects as we're inside ctor 
   }
   void changexy() const 
   {
    // x = 4; //this won't compile because x is not mutable 
    y = 4; //this will compile because y is marked mutable 
   }
};

const C c; //this uses the ctor which can change value of x as well as y
int main()
{
     c.changexy();
}
</code></pre>

